			<div class="content" id="post-content-4838776">
									

<p>Hello! I'm sorry for responding so late. I'll reply to each of these in turn.</p>
<p>Thank you, Zyn. I am new to the site and I appreciate feedback on thread structure. I will keep those critiques in mind in this reply.</p>
<p>For anyone who is interested in checking it out, here is the sandbox link so you can get info on Jeanne's House in greater detail:<br />
<a href="http://scp-sandbox-3.wikidot.com/ohthehumanity">http://scp-sandbox-3.wikidot.com/ohthehumanity</a><br />
(here I refer to Jeanne's home as SCP-6000.)</p>
<p>Dear Duganist,<br />
Your suggestion for &quot;The creepy part&quot; is much appreciated, as it gives me a lot of ideas. Something similar was expressed in the Marionette's self-disassembly to avoid initial containment, That Other Guy's constant invisibility, et cetera, where they each use their individual abilities to rebel against the Foundation. By far the item with the worst thing in store for the researchers is the Dream Garden, which is very particular about friends and foes, and can introduce <em>nightmares</em> to remove the latter (though, of course, it has yet to do so.) Eventually the House itself will begin to repel the constant attempts at containment in a very &quot;Monster House&quot; kind of way (if you've ever seen that movie.)</p>
<p>Each of the residents are unique, and most probably are deserving of their own SCP designations, but for ease the researchers included them as subsets of &quot;SCP-6000.&quot; Discussion of the characters and answers to your questions are located here:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>What are the characters' names, personalities, ages?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Jeanne (Personality: Group mom. Constantly worries she's failing at taking care of the people she cares about.)<br />
Glass Kitten (Pepper, Personality: Cat)<br />
Wooden Marionette (Referred to as &quot;Mendigo,&quot; Personality: Determined. Dry sense of humor.)<br />
Clockwork Dragon (referred to as &quot;Dragon,&quot; Personality: Cat)<br />
The Triple Quartet (3 people: Jamie, Angelica, and Erin. Personalities in that order: Tomboy, Artsy, Nature person.)<br />
Change (Rupert, Personality: Group Dad. Does not, in fact, have a romantic relationship with Jeanne. He's just the group Dad.)<br />
True Empathy (Emily, Personality: Friendly, very approachable. Sometimes melodramatic, but not often.)<br />
The Dream Garden (Personality: It's more of an object then an intelligence, but the thing will respond with lethal force the moment something hostile to its &quot;owners&quot; enter. So I guess it cares about its family?)<br />
That Other Guy (Stephen, Personality: Reclusive. If you get to know him, he's <em>really</em> funny.)<br />
Sad Cloud (Anatoli, Personality: Ironically, he's always happy.)<br />
Murphy's Law (Murphy lol, Personality: Pretty laid-back, kind of &quot;It is what it is&quot; kind of guy.)<br />
Multiple Person Disorder (Lauren, Personality: Kind of a false laid-back. She acts like she's cool with everything but she's just constantly worried about the effects of her anomalous ability kicking in randomly.)<br />
Really Fast (Theo, Personality: Cute kid. He's the overly innocent one of the bunch.)<br />
The House (referred to as &quot;House,&quot; Personality: Benevolent force that cares deeply about its charges. Think of it like the kind old man who loves kids.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What are their DnD alignments?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>All are variants of neutral and good, with none expressing particularly evil tendencies. This may be, however, a result of the House's calming. I haven't thought too much into that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What are their wardrobe choices and how do these reflect their character?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'll write a google doc about this eventually. I'm not too much of an artist but I can also attempt to make portraits for all of them.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How are the relations between the characters? Do they like each other?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yep, they're just about as tight-knit as a normal family should be.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In what state are the characters at the start, happy-ish right?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The characters are pretty happy. They've been living mostly normal lives with normal friends previous to the Foundation's appearance. The children of the group are homeschooled by Jeanne, Rupert (&quot;Change&quot;) and Emily (&quot;True Empathy&quot;).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>A more detailed discussion of the story is located here:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>What change triggers the story? Where and what exactly is the point of no return (nothing will ever be the same). How does this change the characters? Do they turn violent? Does the case researcher lose faith in the Foundation by the end?</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Warning. The following is purely speculative and is not mentioned in the current Foundation report.</strong><br />
Good question. I'm enjoying this. There will come a point in the House vs. Foundation skirmishes that cross a line, and when that happens the House will decide that it's <strong>time to move on</strong>. Doctor John Hammond (due to his feelings for Jeanne) has been trying to act like a mediator between the sides and is, indeed, beginning to worry that the Foundation is taking things too far. His refusal to comply with orders from higher-ups will probably result in his termination, and that might be the catalyst for the House's decision. <strong>I plan on writing a tale about this at some point, which will flesh out this story in a way that wouldn't be possible in the cold, sterile SCP report.</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Does the exploration log of the MTF deployed to take them in have anything reminiscent of war crimes committed in Iraq (or many other places)?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>At first I was confused by this question, but I think I understand. Just conceptualizing here, MTF mistreatment of the residents could result in the beginnings of the skirmishes, and that MTF could serve as the antagonists of the story. Interesting&#8230;</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>In all, thank you for your response. If you are interested in batting ideas back and forth (as a sort of brainstorming session) I would greatly enjoy doing so.</p>
<p>(Edit: My myriad edits have simply been to correct spelling errors and improve structuring, so, if it looks like I'm deleting things they've probably just been moved to the collapsibles)</p>

							</div>

								
					
